{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":0},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The data is there. Now let me look at the 5.NF standards and 4.NF, 3.NF standards since all three errors relate to fractions (Number and Operations—Fractions domain).\n\nError 1: 2/3 + 1/4 computed by adding numerators and denominators directly → violates 5.NF.1 (add/subtract fractions with unlike denominators, including mixed numbers by replacing given fractions with equivalent fractions) and possibly 4.NF.3 (understand a/b + c/d... actually 4.NF.3d is word problems; 4.NF.3 is about decomposing fractions, and understanding addition as joining). Also 4.NF.1 (equivalent fractions) is the underlying prerequisite for common denominators.\n\nError 2: Can't judge whether 3/4 × 5 is bigger or smaller than 5 → violates 5.NF.5 (multiplication as scaling including resizing), specifically 5.NF.5b (explaining why multiplying by fraction > 1 or < 1 changes size). Actually for 3/4 × 5, comparing to 5 requires understanding that multiplying by 3/4 (<1) gives a result less than 5. 5.NF.5a is about comparing size of product to factors without multiplying. Also 4.NF.4 (multiplying fraction by whole number, 4.NF.4b) is prerequisite.\n\nError 3: \"1/2 meter's 1/3\" (1/3 of 1/2 meter) written as 1/2 ÷ 1/3 → violates 5.NF.6 (solve real world problems involving multiplication of fractions) and/or 5.NF.4a, 5.NF.4b. Actually the error is confusing \"of\" (multiplication) with division. 5.NF.6 is real-world multiplication of fractions and mixed numbers. Also 5.NF.7 (division of unit fractions) is what the student incorrectly used. The core standard: 5.NF.4 (apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication to multiply a fraction or whole number by a fraction), 5.NF.4b (find area of rectangle with fractional side lengths), 5.NF.6.\n\nLet me now query the CSV for all the relevant standards. First let me see all 5.NF, 4.NF, 3.NF rows."}}
