 |\n| 5 | Operations and Algebraic Thinking | 3 | 5.OA.1 … 5.OA.3 |\n| 5 | Number and Operations in Base Ten | 7 | 5.NBT.1 … 5.NBT.7 |\n| 5 | Number and Operations—Fractions | 7 | 5.NF.1 … 5.NF.7 |\n| 5 | Measurement and Data | 5 | 5.MD.1 … 5.MD.5 |\n| 5 | Geometry | 4 | 5.G.1 … 5.G.4 |\n| 6 | Ratios and Proportional Relationships | 3 | 6.RP.1 … 6.RP.3 |\n| 6 | The Number System | 8 | 6.NS.1 … 6.NS.8 |\n| 6 | Expressions and Equations | 9 | 6.EE.1 … 6.EE.9 |\n| 6 | Geometry | 4 | 6.G.1 … 6.G.4 |\n| 6 | Statistics and Probability | 5 | 6.SP.1 … 6.SP.5 |\n| 7 | Ratios and Proportional Relationships | 3 | 7.RP.1 … 7.RP.3 |\n| 7 | The Number System | 3 | 7.NS.1 … 7.NS.3 |\n| 7 | Expressions and Equations | 4 | 7.EE.1 … 7.EE.4 |\n| 7 | Geometry | 6 | 7.G.1 … 7.G.6 |\n| 7 | Statistics and Probability | 8 | 7.SP.1 … 7.SP.8 |\n| 8 | The Number System | 2 | 8.NS.1 … 8.NS.2 |\n| 8 | Expressions and Equations | 8 | 8.EE.1 … 8.EE.8 |\n| 8 | Functions | 5 | 8.F.1 … 8.F.5 |\n| 8 | Geometry | 9 | 8.G.1 … 8.G.9 |\n| 8 | Statistics and Probability | 4 | 8.SP.1 … 8.SP.4 |\n| High School — Number and Quantity | The Real Number System | 3 | N-RN.1 … N-RN.3 |\n| High School — Number and Quantity | Quantities★ | 3 | N-Q.1 … N-Q.3 |\n| High School — Number and Quantity | The Complex Number System | 9 | N-CN.1 … N-CN.9 |\n| High School — Number and Quantity | Vector and Matrix Quantities | 12 | N-VM.1 … N-VM.12 |\n| High School — Algebra | Seeing Structure in Expressions | 4 | A-SSE.1 … A-SSE.4 |\n| High School — Algebra | Arithmetic with Polynomials and Rational Expressions | 7 | A-APR.1 … A-APR.7 |\n| High School — Algebra | Creating Equations★ | 4 | A-CED.1 … A-CED.4 |\n| High School — Algebra | Reasoning with Equations and Inequalities | 12 | A-REI.1 … A-REI.12 |\n| High School — Functions | Interpreting Functions | 9 | F-IF.1 … F-IF.9 |\n| High School — Functions | Building Functions | 5 | F-BF.1 … F-BF.5 |\n| High School — Functions | Linear, Quadratic, and Exponential Models★ | 5 | F-LE.1 … F-LE.5 |\n| High School — Functions | Trigonometric Functions | 9 | F-TF.1 … F-TF.9 |\n| High School — Geometry | Congruence | 13 | G-CO.1 … G-CO.13 |\n| High School — Geometry | Similarity, Right Triangles, and Trigonometry | 11 | G-SRT.1 … G-SRT.11 |\n| High School — Geometry | Circles | 5 | G-C.1 … G-C.5 |\n| High School — Geometry | Expressing Geometric Properties with Equations | 7 | G-GPE.1 … G-GPE.7 |\n| High School — Geometry | Geometric Measurement and Dimension | 4 | G-GMD.1 … G-GMD.4 |\n| High School — Geometry | Modeling with Geometry | 3 | G-MG.1 … G-MG.3 |\n| High School — Statistics and Probability | Interpreting Categorical and Quantitative Data | 9 | S-ID.1 … S-ID.9 |\n| High School — Statistics and Probability | Making Inferences and Justifying Conclusions | 6 | S-IC.1 … S-IC.6 |\n| High School — Statistics and Probability | Conditional Probability and the Rules of Probability | 9 | S-CP.1 … S-CP.9 |\n| High School — Statistics and Probability | Using Probability to Make Decisions | 7 | S-MD.1 … S-MD.7 |\n\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788753440783},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_021b5e0c35df4a9393723007","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"code,grade,domain,cluster,requirement,pdf_page\r\nK.CC.1,K,Counting and Cardinality,Know number names and the count sequence.,1. Count to 100 by ones and by tens.,P11\r\nK.CC.2,K,Counting and Cardinality,Know number names and the count sequence.,2. Count forward beginning from a given number within the known sequence (instead of having to begin at 1).,P11\r\nK.CC.3,K,Counting and Cardinality,Know number names and the count sequence.,3. Write numbers from 0 to 20. Represent a number of objects with a written numeral 0-20 (with 0 representing a count of no objects).,P11\r\nK.CC.4,K,Counting and Cardinality,Count to tell the number of objects.,\"4. Understand the relationship between numbers and quantities; connect counting to cardinality. a. When counting objects, say the number names in the standard order, pairing each object with one and only one number name and each number name with one and only one object. b. Understand that the last number name said tells the number of objects counted. The number of objects is the same regardless of their arrangement or the order in which they were counted. c. Understand that each successive number name refers to a quantity that is one larger.\",P11\r\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788753440783},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The data is there. Now let me look at the 5.NF standards and 4.NF, 3.NF standards since all three errors relate to fractions (Number and Operations—Fractions domain).\n\nError 1: 2/3 + 1/4 computed by adding numerators and denominators directly → violates 5.NF.1 (add/subtract fractions with unlike denominators, including mixed numbers by replacing given fractions with equivalent fractions) and possibly 4.NF.3 (understand a/b + c/d... actually 4.NF.3d is word problems; 4.NF.3 is about decomposing fractions, and understanding addition as joining). Also 4.NF.1 (equivalent fractions) is the underlying prerequisite for common denominators.\n\nError 2: Can't judge whether 3/4 × 5 is bigger or smaller than 5 → violates 5.NF.5 (multiplication as scaling including resizing), specifically 5.NF.5b (explaining why multiplying by fraction > 1 or < 1 changes size). Actually for 3/4 × 5, comparing to 5 requires understanding that multiplying by 3/4 (<1) gives a result less than 5. 5.NF.5a is about comparing size of product to factors without multiplying. Also 4.NF.4 (multiplying fraction by whole number, 4.NF.4b) is prerequisite.\n\nError 3: \"1/2 meter's 1/3\" (1/3 of 1/2 meter) written as 1/2 ÷ 1/3 → violates 5.NF.6 (solve real world problems involving multiplication of fractions) and/or 5.NF.4a, 5.NF.4b. Actually the error is confusing \"of\" (multiplication) with division. 5.NF.6 is real-world multiplication of fractions and mixed numbers. Also 5.NF.7 (division of unit fractions) is what the student incorrectly used. The core standard: 5.NF.4 (apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication to multiply a fraction or whole number by a fraction), 5.NF.4b (find area of rectangle with fractional side lengths), 5.NF.6.\n\nLet me now query the CSV for all the relevant standards. First let me see all 5.NF, 4.NF, 3.NF rows.","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"现在我来调取三年级到五年级的所有分数课标，因为这三个错误都发生在 Number and Operations—Fractions（分数的运算与思考）这个领域："},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3c2737a350774ba6a4786ef4","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"grep -E '^[345]\\.NF\\.' /Users/l/other_git_repos/calculus-quest/nodejs_react_lib/learn-claude-code/custom_skill_space/math-standards/references/standards.csv"}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"ark-plan","model":"glm-5.3","usage":{"input":2374,"output":607,"cacheRead":3008,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":5989,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0},"cacheWrite1h":0},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788753440784,"responseId":"0217887534408527820404c56ac6dd9ce215ba8b602495af23696","rawStopReason":"tool_use"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3c2737a350774ba6a4786ef4","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3.NF.1,3,Number and Operations—Fractions[5],Develop understanding of fractions as numbers.,1. Understand a fraction 1/b as the quantity formed by 1 part when a whole is partitioned into b equal parts; understand a fraction a/b as the quantity formed by a parts of size 1/b.,P24\r\n3.NF.2,3,Number and Operations—Fractions[5],Develop understanding of fractions as numbers.,2. Understand a fraction as a number on the number line; represent fractions on a number line diagram. a. Represent a fraction 1/b on a number line diagram by defining the interval from 0 to 1 as the whole and partitioning it into b equal parts. Recognize that each part has size 1/b and that the endpoint of the part based at 0 locates the number 1/b on the number line. b. Represent a fraction a/b on a number line diagram by marking off a lengths 1/b from 0. Recognize that the resulting interval has size a/b and that its endpoint locates the number a/b on the number line.,P24\r\n3.NF.3,3,Number and Operations—Fractions[5],Develop understanding of fractions as numbers.,\"3. Explain equivalence of fractions in special cases, and compare fractions by reasoning about their size. a. Understand two fractions as equivalent (equal) if they are the same size, or the same point on a number line. b. Recognize and generate simple equivalent fractions, c. Express whole numbers as fractions, and recognize fractions that are equivalent to whole numbers. d. Compare two fractions with the same numerator or the same denominator by reasoning about their size. Recognize that comparisons are valid only when the two fractions refer to the same whole. Record the results of comparisons with the symbols >, =, or <, and justify the conclusions,\",P24\r\n4.NF.1,4,Number and Operations—Fractions[3],Extend understanding of fraction equivalence and ordering.,\"1. Explain why a fraction a/b is equivalent to a fraction (n × a)/(n × b) by using visual fraction models, with attention to how the number and size of the parts differ even though the two fractions themselves are the same size. Use this principle to recognize and generate equivalent fractions.\",P30\r\n4.NF.2,4,Number and Operations—Fractions[3],Extend understanding of fraction equivalence and ordering.,\"2. Compare two fractions with different numerators and different denominators,\",P30\r\n4.NF.3,4,Number and Operations—Fractions[3],Build fractions from unit fractions by applying and extending previous understandings of operations on whole numbers.,\"3. Understand a fraction a/b with a > 1 as a sum of fractions 1/b. a. Understand addition and subtraction of fractions as joining and separating parts referring to the same whole. b. Decompose a fraction into a sum of fractions with the same denominator in more than one way, recording each decomposition by an equation. Justify decompositions, c. Add and subtract mixed numbers with like denominators, d. Solve word problems involving addition and subtraction of fractions referring to the same whole and having like denominators,\",P30\r\n4.NF.4,4,Number and Operations—Fractions[3],Build fractions from unit fractions by applying and extending previous understandings of operations on whole numbers.,\"4. Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication to multiply a fraction by a whole number. a. Understand a fraction a/b as a multiple of 1/b. b. Understand a multiple of a/b as a multiple of 1/b, and use this understanding to multiply a fraction by a whole number. c. Solve word problems involving multiplication of a fraction by a whole number,\",P30\r\n4.NF.5,4,Number and Operations—Fractions[3],\"Understand decimal notation for fractions, and compare decimal fractions.\",\"5. Express a fraction with denominator 10 as an equivalent fraction with denominator 100, and use this technique to add two fractions with respective denominators 10 and 100.4\",P31\r\n4.NF.6,4,Number and Operations—Fractions[3],\"Understand decimal notation for fractions, and compare decimal fractions.\",6. Use decimal notation for fractions with denominators 10 or 100.,P31\r\n4.NF.7,4,Number and Operations—Fractions[3],\"Understand decimal notation for fractions, and compare decimal fractions.\",\"7. Compare two decimals to hundredths by reasoning about their size. Recognize that comparisons are valid only when the two decimals refer to the same whole. Record the results of comparisons with the symbols >, =, or <, and justify the conclusions,\",P31\r\n5.NF.1,5,Number and Operations—Fractions,Use equivalent fractions as a strategy to add and subtract fractions.,1. Add and subtract fractions with unlike denominators (including mixed numbers) by replacing given fractions with equivalent fractions in such a way as to produce an equivalent sum or difference of fractions with like denominators.,P36\r\n5.NF.2,5,Number and Operations—Fractions,Use equivalent fractions as a strategy to add and subtract fractions.,\"2. Solve word problems involving addition and subtraction of fractions referring to the same whole, including cases of unlike denominators,\",P36\r\n5.NF.3,5,Number and Operations—Fractions,Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication and division to multiply and divide fractions.,\"3. Interpret a fraction as division of the numerator by the denominator (a/b = a ÷ b). Solve word problems involving division of whole numbers leading to answers in the form of fractions or mixed numbers,\",\"P36, 37\"\r\n5.NF.4,5,Number and Operations—Fractions,Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication and division to multiply and divide fractions.,\"4. Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication to multiply a fraction or whole number by a fraction. a. Interpret the product (a/b) × q as a parts of a partition of q into b equal parts; equivalently, as the result of a sequence of operations a × q ÷ b. b. Find the area of a rectangle with fractional side lengths by tiling it with unit squares of the appropriate unit fraction side lengths, and show that the area is the same as would be found by multiplying the side lengths. Multiply fractional side lengths to find areas of rectangles, and represent fraction products as rectangular areas.\",\"P36, 37\"\r\n5.NF.5,5,Number and Operations—Fractions,Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication and division to multiply and divide fractions.,\"5. Interpret multiplication as scaling (resizing), by: a. Comparing the size of a product to the size of one factor on the basis of the size of the other factor, without performing the indicated multiplication. b. Explaining why multiplying a given number by a fraction greater than 1 results in a product greater than the given number (recognizing multiplication by whole numbers greater than 1 as a familiar case); explaining why multiplying a given number by a fraction less than 1 results in a product smaller than the given number; and relating the principle of fraction equivalence a/b = (n×a)/(n×b) to the effect of multiplying a/b by 1.\",\"P36, 37\"\r\n5.NF.6,5,Number and Operations—Fractions,Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication and division to multiply and divide fractions.,\"6. Solve real world problems involving multiplication of fractions and mixed numbers,\",\"P36, 37\"\r\n5.NF.7,5,Number and Operations—Fractions,Apply and extend previous understandings of multiplication and division to multiply and divide fractions.,\"7. Apply and extend previous understandings of division to divide unit fractions by whole numbers and whole numbers by unit fractions. a. Interpret division of a unit fraction by a non-zero whole number, and compute such quotients. b. Interpret division of a whole number by a unit fraction, and compute such quotients. c. Solve real world problems involving division of unit fractions by non-zero whole numbers and division of whole numbers by unit fractions,\",\"P36, 37\"\r\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788753451428},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I have all the data I need. Let me also check the OA (Operations and Algebraic Thinking) chain, particularly for the \"of\" meaning multiplication — that goes back to 4.OA.1 (multiplicative comparison) or 3.OA.1 (interpret products). Actually, \"1/2 of 1/3\" relates to 5.NF.4a. But the deeper prerequisite for \"of = multiply\" is... In Common Core, 3.OA.1 is \"Interpret products of whole numbers, e.g., interpret 5 × 7 as the total number of objects in 5 groups of 7 objects each.\" That's about equal groups. The \"fraction of a quantity\" is directly 5.NF.4a.\n\nBut the user said \"沿同一 domain 往低年级追出前置课标链\" — trace back prerequisite chains along the same domain. The domain is Number and Operations—Fractions, which starts at grade 3. So the chain within the NF domain:\n\nFor error 1 (2/3 + 1/4):\n- 5.NF.1 (add/subtract unlike denominators via equivalent fractions)\n- 4.NF.1 (equivalent fractions)\n- 4.NF.3 (understand a/b as sum of unit fractions; addition as joining parts referring to the same whole — a and b: like denominators add/subtract)\n- 4.NF.2 (compare fractions with different numerators/denominators — relevant since student needs to compare? Not core)\n- 3.NF.1 (unit fraction concept)\n- 3.NF.3 (equivalence in special cases)\n\nFor error 2 (3/4 × 5 vs 5):\n- 5.NF.5 (multiplication as scaling, 5.NF.5a comparing size of product to size of one factor without multiplying; 5.NF.5b explaining why multiplying by fraction < 1 gives smaller product)\n- 4.NF.4 (multiply fraction by whole number, 4.NF.4b)\n- 4.NF.1 (equivalence)\n- 3.NF.1, 3.NF.3 (also 3.NF.3d compare fractions — knowing 3/4 < 1 requires comparing fractions to benchmark... 3.NF.3d is comparing two fractions with same numerator/denominator; 3.NF.3c whole numbers as fractions, e.g., 1 = 4/4)\n\nFor error 3 (1/2 meter's 1/3 → 1/2 ÷ 1/3):\n- 5.NF.6 (real world problems involving multiplication of fractions) — \"1/2 of 1/3 meter\" is multiplication of two fractions\n- 5.NF.4a (interpret (a/b) × q as a parts of partition of q into b equal parts)\n- 4.NF.4c (word problems involving multiplication of fraction by whole number)\n- Also 5.NF.7 is the division standard the student confused — but that's the incorrect target, not the violated one. The violated one is 5.NF.4a/5.NF.6.\n- 3.NF.1 (unit fraction as quantity formed by 1 part when whole partitioned into b equal parts)\n\nLet me also verify the pdf_page values:\n- 5.NF.1: P36\n- 5.NF.5: P36, 37\n- 5.NF.6: P36, 37\n- 5.NF.4: P36, 37\n- 4.NF.1: P30\n- 4.NF.2: P30\n- 4.NF.3: P30\n- 4.NF.4: P30\n- 3.NF.1: P24\n- 3.NF.2: P24\n- 3.NF.3: P24\n\nAll real codes. The user says \"只引用表里真实存在的编号\" — only cite codes that actually exist in the table. All the ones I listed are in the table (I just saw them in the grep output).\n\nNow for the prerequisite chain along the same domain (NF domain), going from grade 5 down to grade 3 (the NF domain starts at grade 3; before that there's no NF domain — K-2 have no fractions domain).\n\nWait, should I also include 3.NF.2 (number line)? It's helpful for remediation but the core chain for these errors:\n\nError 1 chain (addition with unlike denominators):\n- 5.NF.1 ← requires 4.NF.3 (join/separate parts of same whole, add like denominators, decompose) ← requires 4.NF.1 (equivalent fractions) ← requires 3.NF.3 (equivalence in special cases) ← requires 3.NF.1 (unit fraction / a/b as a parts of 1/b)\n\nError 2 chain (scaling/comparison):\n- 5.NF.5 ← requires 4.NF.4 (fraction × whole number) ← requires 4.NF.1 ← requires 3.NF.3 (3.NF.3c express whole numbers as fractions; 3.NF.3d compare fractions) ← requires 3.NF.1\n\nError 3 chain (fraction of a quantity in word problems):\n- 5.NF.6 / 5.NF.4a ← requires 4.NF.4c (word problems fraction × whole number) ← requires 4.NF.4a/b ← requires 3.NF.1/3.NF.2\n\nAlso 5.NF.3 (fraction as division a/b = a ÷ b) is worth mentioning for error 3 — the student confused \"1/2 of 1/3\" with division. Actually the confusion may stem from not understanding fraction-of as multiplication. 5.NF.3 is about interpreting fractions as division, which is a different concept. The error is writing 1/2 ÷ 1/3 for \"1/2 米的 1/3\" (1/3 of 1/2 meter). The correct expression is 1/2 × 1/3. The violated standard is 5.NF.4a / 5.NF.6. Could also mention that the student is misapplying division ideas covered in 5.NF.7.\n\nHmm, should I also check 5.NF.2? Error 1 could also involve 5.NF.2 (word problems with unlike denominators) but the presented error is pure computation, so 5.NF.1 is the direct match.\n\nAlso worth noting: the \"add numerators, add denominators\" error specifically violates the understanding in 4.NF.3a (\"addition and subtraction of fractions as joining and separating parts referring to the same whole\") — the student added parts of different-sized wholes. And 4.NF.1/4.NF.3b justify why you can't just add numerators and denominators (parts must refer to the same whole / same unit fraction).\n\nLet me structure the answer:\n\n## 一、错题定位（违反的课标）\n\n**错误 1: 2/3 + 1/4 = (2+1)/(3+4)**\n- 直接违反 5.NF.1 (5年级, NF, P36): \"Add and subtract fractions with unlike denominators (including mixed numbers) by replacing given fractions with equivalent fractions in such a way as to produce an equivalent sum or difference of fractions with like denominators.\"\n- 深层违反 4.NF.3a (4年级, P30): \"Understand addition and subtraction of fractions as joining and separating parts referring to the same whole.\" — the student joined parts of different-sized units.\n- 辅助：4.NF.1 (P30) equivalent fractions — 2/3 = 8/12, 1/4 = 3/12.\n\n**错误 2: 3/4 × 5 与 5 比大小**\n- 直接违反 5.NF.5a/b (P36-37): \"a. Comparing the size of a product to the size of one factor on the basis of the size of the other factor, without performing the indicated multiplication. b. ... explaining why multiplying a given number by a fraction less than 1 results in a product smaller than the given number...\"\n- 3/4 < 1, so 3/4 × 5 < 5. The student cannot reason this without multiplying — exactly what 5.NF.5a targets.\n\n**错误 3: 「1/2 米的 1/3」列成 1/2 ÷ 1/3**\n- 直接违反 5.NF.6 (P36-37): \"Solve real world problems involving multiplication of fractions and mixed numbers\" — \"的\" (of) structure should be multiplication 1/2 × 1/3.\n- 概念性违反 5.NF.4a (P36-37): \"Interpret the product (a/b) × q as a parts of a partition of q into b equal parts\" — 1/2米的1/3 = partition 1/2 meter into 3 equal parts, take 1 part.\n- (误用了 5.NF.7 的除法 — 可顺带指出正确目标是乘法)\n\n## 二、沿 NF domain 往低年级的前置链\n\nGrade 5 → 4 → 3 (NF domain 从 3 年级才开始):\n\nAddition chain (错误1):\n- 5.NF.1 (5) ← 4.NF.3a/b/c (4) ← 4.NF.1 (4) ← 3.NF.3a/b (3) ← 3.NF.1 (3)\n\nScaling chain (错误2):\n- 5.NF.5a/b (5) ← 4.NF.4a/b (4) ← 4.NF.1 (4) ← 3.NF.3c/d (3) ← 3.NF.1 (3)\n\nMultiplication-of-fractions chain (错误3):\n- 5.NF.6 (5) ← 5.NF.4a (5) ← 4.NF.4c (4) ← 4.NF.4a (4) ← 3.NF.2 (3) ← 3.NF.1 (3)\n\nCommon root: 3.NF.1 (unit fraction), possibly 3.NF.2 (number line).\n\n## 三、补救顺序（从最底层前置开始）\n\n1. 3.NF.1 — unit fraction concept: partitioning a whole into b equal parts, a/b = a parts of 1/b. Use area/length models. (这是三个错误的共同根)\n2. 3.NF.2 — number line representation (optional but helps; maybe include as 1.5)\n3. 3.NF.3 — equivalence in special cases (a/b same size = equal; whole as fraction 4/4; compare with same numerator/denominator)\n4. 4.NF.1 — equivalent fractions (n×a)/(n×b) with visual models → enables common denominators\n5. 4.NF.3a/b — join/separate parts referring to same whole; decompose with like denominators → fixes \"add numerators & denominators\" error: only same-size parts can be counted together\n6. 4.NF.4a/b/c — fraction × whole number, as multiple of unit fraction; word problems\n7. 5.NF.4a — (a/b) × q interpretation; 5.NF.5a/b scaling; 5.NF.1 unlike denominators; 5.NF.6 word problems\n\nOrder rationale: build from unit fraction → equivalence → same-whole addition → multiplication → scaling → real world problems.\n\nActually let me think about the remediation order more carefully:\n\nStage 1 (3rd grade foundation):\n- 3.NF.1: unit fractions — a/b means a parts of 1/b. Without this, nothing works.\n- 3.NF.2: number line (helps but not strictly required by the three errors; I can include it as it's in the same domain and supports understanding. But the user said only cite codes that exist — it does exist. Including it is fine, but maybe keep it lean. I'll include it briefly.)\n- 3.NF.3: equivalence basics + comparison — root of both 4.NF.1 and 5.NF.5 reasoning (knowing 3/4 < 1 needs comparing to whole 4/4, which is 3.NF.3c).\n\nStage 2 (4th grade):\n- 4.NF.1: equivalent fractions → common denominators\n- 4.NF.3a/b: add/subtract like denominators, same whole — directly counters \"numerator+numerator, denominator+denominator\"\n- 4.NF.4a/b/c: fr